SUMMARY:Semantics and intermediate representations of probabilistic program
 s (Hugo Paquet)
LOCATION:LG23\, Computer Science
DESCRIPTION:Hugo Paquet\nTheoretical Computer Science Seminar\n\nIn a proba
 bilistic programming language (with only finite types and without recursio
 n or loops) every program determines a probability distribution over a set
  of outputs. Computing this distribution can be expensive: the naive appro
 ach is to enumerate all possible executions. So practical interpreters rel
 y on more efficient representations of probability distributions\, typical
 ly inspired by symbolic execution. In this talk I will give a semantic per
 spective on these intermediate representations\, to explain why they are c
 orrect. The key idea is to decompose probabilistic sampling into two separ
 ate effects (first generating a random variable\, and then reading its val
 ue) that follow different…\n\nDetails: https://researchseminars.org/talk
 /TheoryCSBham/85/
